# **What this document is and how to use it**

The Digital Skills Framework is a reference document I have developed to create a shared language for digital skills conversations across all 30 curriculum areas at Weston College. It maps each area to the DfE Essential Digital Skills categories and to the industry specific tools that employers in those sectors actually use.

It is not a policy document. It is a coaching tool. I open it at the relevant area in every Head of Area meeting, and I use what is there as a conversation starter rather than a conclusion. The question I am asking is: does any of this already happen in your area? If so, where, and is it being named and evidenced as digital skills development? If not, is it something that should be, and what would it take?

| **The principle I keep coming back to** Never teach the technology. Teach the task that technology enables. A construction learner does not need to learn about QR codes. They need to clock in on site, submit a digital site diary, and order materials from a supplier portal. The apps are how they do it. When it is framed that way, digital stops being a separate burden and becomes part of the fabric of vocational practice. |
| --- |

The framework is structured in eight sections. This document covers all eight in full. Section 3, the full 30 area curriculum mapping, is the section I use most frequently in coaching conversations. It is proposed mapping, not audit findings. Heads of Area will confirm, challenge, and add to it as conversations develop.

# **Section 1: The Top 10 Digital Skills for Employment and Life**

**2026 Universal Baseline**

These ten skills represent the universal baseline that every learner, employee, and citizen needs in 2026. They are drawn from three sources: the DfE Essential Digital Skills Framework, the FutureDotNow AI updated EDS from October 2025, and the World Economic Forum Future of Jobs Report 2025.

| **Rank** | **Skill** | **What it means in practice** | **Category** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 01 | AI Literacy and Prompt Skills | Understanding what AI tools are, how to use them safely and ethically, how to write effective prompts, and how to critically evaluate AI generated outputs rather than accept them | Emerging Priority |
| 02 | Digital Communication and Collaboration | Effective and professional use of email, messaging, video calls, and shared document platforms in learning and workplace contexts, not just recreational use | Universal |
| 03 | Information and Data Handling | Finding reliable information, evaluating sources critically, organising data, and using it to make evidence based decisions or produce evidence based work | Core EDS |
| 04 | Online Safety and Cybersecurity | Strong passwords, recognising phishing, enabling two factor authentication, understanding privacy settings, protecting personal and organisational data | Safety |
| 05 | Digital Productivity Tools | Confident use of word processing, spreadsheets, presentations, calendars, and cloud storage for work and study. Often a much bigger assumption of competency than it should be. | Core EDS |
| 06 | Problem Solving with Technology | Using digital tools to find solutions, troubleshoot issues, and adapt to new software without needing step by step guidance every time | Applied |
| 07 | Digital Transactions and Services | Online payments, digital forms, tax returns, booking appointments, and using government and financial services independently | Life and Work |
| 08 | Creating and Sharing Digital Content | Producing presentations, documents, short videos, infographics, and professional social media posts for defined purposes and real audiences | Creative |
| 09 | Virtual Collaboration and Remote Working | Working effectively in digitally mediated environments: Teams, Zoom, shared cloud documents, digital project management, and professional digital etiquette | Work |
| 10 | Digital Wellbeing and Inclusion | Maintaining healthy screen habits, understanding technology's impact on wellbeing, and designing or using accessible digital content that works for everyone | Inclusion |

Sources: DfE Essential Digital Skills Framework (gov.uk/government/publications/essential-digital-skills-framework) | FutureDotNow AI Updated EDS October 2025 (futuredotnow.uk) | WEF Future of Jobs 2025 (weforum.org)

# **Section 2: General Digital Skills**

**For Learning, For Work, For Society**

The five EDS categories applied across three contexts. I use this section when a Head of Area says their area does not do much digital. It helps to show that digital skills for learning and life are already embedded in most provision, even when they are not named as such. The five categories are: Communicating; Handling Information and Content; Transacting; Problem Solving; Being Safe and Legal Online.

## **For Learning**

| **Skill** | **What it looks like** | **EDS Category** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Navigating digital platforms | Using VLEs, Microsoft Teams, SharePoint, and college portals to access timetables, resources, and learning materials independently, not just when prompted | Problem Solving |
| Research and information literacy | Searching effectively using specific terms and multiple sources. Evaluating source credibility, publication dates, and author authority. Identifying AI generated misinformation, a much bigger challenge than it was two years ago. | Handling Information |
| Digital note taking and organisation | Using OneNote, Google Docs, or equivalent for organised, retrievable notes. Creating folder structures and naming conventions. Managing version history. | Handling Information |
| Collaborative digital working | Co editing shared documents in real time, contributing to group Teams channels, completing peer review tasks digitally, and managing asynchronous collaboration | Communicating |
| AI assisted learning | Using AI tools to support research, drafting, and self quizzing, with full understanding of academic integrity and institutional AI policies. Not passive reliance. Active, critical use. | Handling Information |

## **For the World of Work**

| **Skill** | **What it looks like** | **EDS Category** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Professional communication | Email and Teams etiquette, video call protocol, appropriate tone for digital workplace communication. Knowing when to use email versus a message versus a call. A genuine skill gap in most cohorts. | Communicating |
| Productivity and office applications | Confident use of Word, Excel with formulae, PowerPoint, cloud storage, and Outlook calendar. Understanding how applications can be combined to improve efficiency. | Problem Solving |
| Digital transactions and invoicing | Online payments, basic invoicing via Xero or Sage, submitting VAT returns digitally, managing work related financial records. HMRC Making Tax Digital awareness is now essential for any learner going into self employment. | Transacting |
| Data handling and record keeping | Accurate digital data entry, understanding GDPR obligations, maintaining compliant records, and interpreting basic workplace data | Handling Information; Being Safe and Legal |
| AI literacy for the workplace | Using AI assistants to draft, summarise, and research. Evaluating outputs critically. Understanding ethical use, prompt engineering basics, and the limitations of AI tools. | Handling Information |

## **For Societal Inclusion**

| **Skill** | **What it looks like** | **EDS Category** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Accessing public and government services | Gov.UK digital services, Universal Credit portal, NHS App, local council online forms, HMRC self assessment. These are not optional skills. They are infrastructure for independent living. | Transacting |
| Online safety and privacy | Password managers, two factor authentication, recognising phishing and social engineering, GDPR basics, reviewing social media privacy settings actively | Being Safe and Legal |
| Digital financial inclusion | Online banking, comparing providers digitally, avoiding scams, understanding digital payment systems | Transacting |
| Critical engagement with media | Identifying misinformation, understanding algorithmic bias and filter bubbles, responsible social media use, understanding sponsored versus editorial content | Handling Information |
| Digital wellbeing and accessibility | Screen time management, understanding technology's impact on mental health, using and creating accessible digital content to WCAG 2.2 standards, assistive technology awareness | Being Safe and Legal |

