## **FIP and SEND**

| **Technology is not a learning tool in FIP. It is often the means by which learning is possible at all.** For FIP areas, staff training on assistive technology is not optional CPD. It is a professional standard for everyone working with these cohorts. Proloquo2Go, Tobii eye gaze, Clicker and Widgit, Read and Write: staff need to know what is available and how to use it. This is a direct obligation under our SEND Centre of Excellence heritage and a clear expectation within the renewed Ofsted Inclusion grade. |
| --- |

| **Code** | **Area** | **Core Digital Skills** | **Subject Digital Tools** | **Context** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| FAU | FIP Autism | Predictable structured digital environments; digital routines; AAC communication tools | Proloquo2Go and Grid 3 for AAC; Choiceworks for visual schedules; sensory appropriate digital environments | AAC technologies are central to Autism provision. Consistent Teams layout and clear file naming conventions reduce anxiety significantly. Sudden platform changes without notice must be avoided. |
| FCO | FIP Complex Needs | Switch access technology; eye gaze communication systems; accessible digital devices | Tobii eye gaze; switch access devices; Clicker and Widgit Online; sensory apps and interactive digital surfaces | Technology enables communication and participation that would otherwise be impossible for these learners. Device and software reliability is critical, not peripheral. Staff training is mandatory. |
| FPL | FIP Pre Vocational | Basic device operation; simple task management apps; online communication basics | Read and Write for text to speech and dictation; Immersive Reader; Clicker 8; Taskmaster and visual timetable apps | Pre vocational and pathway provision. Digital daily living tools, travel apps, banking apps, NHS App, support the transition to employment and independent living that this area is designed to enable. |

# **Section 4: Industry Specific Digital Tools**

**What General Qualifications Do Not Cover**

This section is for use when working with Heads of Area to audit specific gaps. The question I ask is: which of these tools do your learners currently use, and which do your placement employers expect them to know? The answers often reveal a gap between what is being taught and what industry is actually looking for.

| **Sector** | **Key Industry Digital Tools** | **Why this matters** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Construction and Built Environment | Revit and AutoCAD for BIM Level 2; Fieldwire and Procore for site management; Site App Pro for QR clock in; laser levels; BREEAM sustainability tools | BIM Level 2 is a legal requirement for all UK government funded construction projects. Digital clock in is standard on every major contract site. |
| Health, Care and Early Years | SystmOne and EMIS for electronic patient records; Liquid Logic; telehealth platforms; Tapestry and Evidence Me for EEY; e Prescribing; digital MAR charts | NHS Digital strategy mandates electronic records across all settings. Caldicott data protection is a legal requirement from day one of employment in care. |
| Engineering and Motor Vehicle | OBD II diagnostic software; Bosch and ALLDATA; EV battery management systems; ADAS calibration tools; SolidWorks and Fusion 360; SAP and Oracle ERP | The EV transition means battery management and ADAS calibration are now critical digital skills for all modern vehicle technicians, not specialist knowledge. |
| Hair, Beauty and Hospitality | Fresha, Treatwell, and Vagaro; OpenTable and ResDiary; EPOS and Square; Natasha's Law allergen software; HMRC Making Tax Digital | HMRC Making Tax Digital makes digital VAT submission mandatory for self employed practitioners from 2026. This is a compliance requirement, not optional awareness. |
| Creative Arts, Games and Media | Adobe Creative Suite; Unity and Unreal Engine; Git version control; AI creative tools including Adobe Firefly and Midjourney, with IP literacy | Adobe CC proficiency is the industry baseline in creative employment. Not having it is not an option for learners going into these industries. |
| Business and IT | Xero, QuickBooks, and Sage; Salesforce and HubSpot; Power BI; Python, SQL, and JavaScript; Azure, AWS, and GitHub; Wireshark and Kali Linux for DCI | WEF Future of Jobs 2025 identifies data analysis and AI literacy as the fastest growing business skills globally for the period 2025 to 2030. |
| Public Services | Niche RMS for police records management; Airwave and digital radio systems; body worn camera evidence management; GIS mapping tools; OSINT tools with ethical and legal grounding | Police, fire, and armed forces all require digital literacy at entry level. These are baseline employability requirements for this sector, not advanced skills. |
| Sport and Active Living | Hudl and Dartfish for video analysis; Catapult and Garmin for wearable GPS data; Trainerize and PT Distinction for coaching platforms; sports booking and facilities management software | Hudl is used by most professional clubs for performance analysis. Wearable tech data interpretation is now expected at graduate entry level in sport science and coaching roles. |

# **Section 5: Framing Digital Skills for Vocational Contexts**

How digital skills are framed makes an enormous difference to whether staff and learners engage with them. What I have found is that the most effective framing always connects the digital skill directly to an industry outcome that the learner already cares about. The technology becomes invisible. It is just how you do the job.

| **Framing Approach** | **What it sounds like** | **When to use it** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The Industry Standard Frame | Every major contractor uses digital clock in. You will not get paid if you cannot sign in on site digitally. Or: EV diagnostics are entirely digital. You cannot fix a modern vehicle without a tablet. The technology is not the subject. The job requirement is. | When a Head of Area or learner says digital is not relevant to their area. Almost always the wrong conclusion. Use Section 4 industry tools to make the case. |
| The Time Saving Frame | Fresha sends appointment reminders automatically and takes deposits. You spend less time chasing no shows and more time with clients. Or: Electronic records mean you do not rewrite the same patient information by hand three times a shift. Digital as workload relief. | When staff are resistant to digital tools because they feel like extra work. Show them the time they get back. |
| The Earnings and Employability Frame | Xero certification adds an estimated three to five thousand pounds to your starting salary as a bookkeeper or finance assistant. Or: Site managers who can use BIM earn significantly more than those who cannot. Connect digital competence to tangible economic outcomes. | When learners or parents question why digital skills matter in a vocational programme. Labour market intelligence makes the case quickly. |
| The Authentic Context Frame | Measure this wall, log the data digitally, and email it to the project manager. The learner has practised data entry, file handling, and professional email in one real task. Or: Book a GP appointment on the NHS App. A real digital service, not a classroom simulation. | This is the most effective framing. The digital skill becomes invisible: the learner is just doing the job. Use this wherever possible, especially for ESOL and community education learners where real world application is the strongest motivator. |

| **The 21CLD connection** The 21st Century Learning Design framework describes knowledge building, real world problem solving, skilled communication, and self regulation as the learning activities most likely to develop the skills learners need for work and life. All of these are most richly realised when digital tools are embedded in authentic vocational tasks rather than taught in isolation. The authentic context framing approach is where these principles are already alive in good vocational teaching. You do not need to name the framework to work within it. |
| --- |

# **Section 6: SEND, Inclusion and Accessibility**

This section underpins the Learning Without Barriers proposal. The principle here is one I feel strongly about: accessibility is not a bolt on for certain learners. The best digital accessibility practice benefits everyone. Captions help in noisy environments. Clear navigation reduces anxiety. Accessible colour contrast helps in poor lighting. Design it in from the start and you serve every learner better.

| **The WCAG 2.2 AA Standard** WCAG 2.2 AA is the internationally recognised standard for accessible digital content and the legally required standard for UK public sector organisations. It should be the minimum standard for every digital resource created by any member of staff. The key requirements are: minimum 4.5:1 colour contrast ratio for normal text, alternative text descriptions on all images, correct heading structure in all documents, captions on all video content, and keyboard navigability. The Microsoft Office Accessibility Checker checks most of these automatically. It is built into every Office application and takes seconds to run. |
| --- |

| **Learner Group** | **Key Considerations** | **Tools and Approaches** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| FIP Autism (FAU) | Many autistic learners have strong digital aptitude. Recognise and build on this in ILPs. Predictable, consistent digital environments reduce anxiety significantly. Sudden platform changes without notice can be genuinely distressing. | Proloquo2Go and Grid 3 for AAC; Choiceworks for visual schedules; consistent Teams layout and file naming conventions; sensory settings adjusted as needed |
| FIP Complex Needs (FCO) | For this cohort, technology is often the primary means of communication and participation. Device and software reliability is not a convenience issue. It is a safeguarding and access issue. Staff training is mandatory. | Tobii eye gaze for learners without motor control; switch access devices; Clicker and Widgit Online; interactive digital surfaces; all college devices must have this software available and functioning |
| FIP Pre Vocational (FPL) | Basic device operation as a foundation for the transition to employment. Digital daily living tools support independence in ways that have direct quality of life implications. | Read and Write for text to speech and dictation; Immersive Reader, free, built into Teams and Word, shown to every learner at induction; Clicker 8; visual timetable apps for routine and structure |
| ESOL Learners (ESO) | Language exclusion and digital exclusion are compounding barriers that need to be addressed together, not in sequence. Microsoft Translator and Google Translate are scaffolding tools that bridge to independence, not dependency supports. | Microsoft Translator and Google Translate as standard scaffolding; Immersive Reader with translation enabled; real world digital tasks such as NHS App, DWP portal, and online banking as the most motivating and immediately useful learning contexts |
| Dyslexia and SpLD (All Areas) | Read and Write Gold, Immersive Reader, OneNote digital inking for spatial note taking, and Dragon Dictate for extended writing tasks. These are productivity tools that happen to be especially effective for learners with literacy difficulties. They should be available to everyone. | Read and Write Gold; Immersive Reader, zero cost and high impact; OneNote digital inking; Dragon Dictate or Windows Speech Recognition; mention all of these at induction as available to all learners, not only those with a recorded need |
| Universal Design (All Areas) | WCAG 2.2 AA compliance for all digital resources created by staff. Captions on all video. Alt text on all images. Accessible colour contrast. Accessibility Checker run before every resource is shared. | WCAG 2.2 Quick Reference at w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref │ Microsoft Accessibility Checker built into all Office applications |

# **Section 7: Digital Skills Progression Ladders**

**Foundation to Highly Advanced | Based on DigComp 3.0 (European Commission, November 2025)**

Two parallel progression ladders. I use these to position learners and staff on a shared scale for CPD planning, digital hours evidence, and self assessment conversations.

## **Ladder 1: General Digital Skills (Learner Facing)**

| **Level** | **What it looks like** | **DigComp 3.0** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Foundation | Can switch on a device, use a touchscreen, and open an app or browser with guidance. Recognises common icons. May not distinguish between search results and adverts. | A1: Newcomer |
| Basic | Can write, send, and reply to emails with attachments. Uses specific search terms. Can create a simple Word document, save it, and retrieve it. Can navigate Gov.UK and book a GP appointment independently. | A2: Explorer |
| Intermediate | Uses Word, Excel with formulae, and PowerPoint confidently. Joins and hosts video calls. Uses strong, unique passwords and two factor authentication. Recognises misinformation. Can use AI assistants and knows to verify their outputs. | B1: Integrator |
| Advanced | Excel pivot tables, Power BI dashboards, digital project management. Uses AI tools strategically to accelerate work and evaluates outputs critically. Understands GDPR obligations. Can guide others in digital tools with pedagogical rationale. | B2: Expert |
| Highly Advanced | Shapes digital culture or strategy at organisational level. Builds automated workflows using Power Automate or Zapier. Identifies novel applications of AI. Advocates for ethical and inclusive digital practice across the institution. | C1 and C2: Leader and Pioneer |

## **Ladder 2: IT Skills for FE and HE (Academic and Professional Contexts)**

| **Level** | **What it looks like** |
| --- | --- |
| Foundation | Can log into college email, Teams, and the VLE with prompting. Can access course materials. Can print from a college PC with guidance. |
| Basic | Accesses assignment briefs, submissions, grades, and feedback via the VLE independently. Uses Microsoft 365 basics. Submits work via Turnitin or Teams Assignment. Joins class Teams channels and attends online sessions. |
| Intermediate | Uses EBSCO or JSTOR with Boolean search. Creates accessible presentations with correct heading structure and alt text. Uses Excel or SPSS for basic data analysis. Maintains an ePortfolio. Uses AI tools with full understanding of institutional AI policy. |
| Advanced | Uses SPSS, R, or Python for statistical analysis. Produces multimedia academic content. Maintains an active professional LinkedIn profile and digital portfolio. Briefs others on data findings. |
| Highly Advanced | Publishes research digitally via open access repositories. Creates OERs for others. Applies computational thinking or scripting to academic or professional problems. Advocates for responsible, inclusive digital practice and contributes to institutional AI and data policy. |

Source: DigComp 3.0, European Commission Joint Research Centre, November 2025 (joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu/digcomp_en) | Jisc Building Digital Capability Framework (digitalcapability.jisc.ac.uk)

# **Section 8: Teacher Digital Skills**

**TLA Focused Progression | ETF Digital Teaching Professional Framework and DigCompEdu**

A structured progression framework for FE and HE teaching staff, from first engaging with technology in the classroom through to leading digital innovation across the college. I use this section in conversations with TLAMs about CPD priorities and Digital Lead development.

| **Stage** | **ETF DTPF** | **What it looks like in practice** | **DigCompEdu** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Stage 1 | Exploring | Beginning to explore digital tools. Relies on guidance. Has attended an overview of key college digital tools but has not yet integrated them meaningfully into regular teaching. Aware of what is available, not yet acting on it. | A1 and A2: Newcomer to Explorer |
| Stage 2A | Adopting: Developing | Uses Teams for communication, basic assignments, and feedback. Aware of the college AI policy. Begins using AI to reduce own workload via quiz generation and lesson plan drafts. Uses formative digital assessment at least occasionally. | B1: Integrator |
| Stage 2B | Adopting: Embedding | Designs lesson sequences where digital tools purposefully change the learning experience, not just present content differently. Applies WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility principles to all digital resources as a professional habit. Uses AI tools critically and transparently. Can articulate which EDS categories their learners are developing and how. | B2: Expert |
| Stage 3 | Leading | Coaches and mentors colleagues on digital pedagogy. Designs digital first curriculum where the VLE is a live, learner directed environment. Evaluates EdTech critically, considering pedagogical rationale, GDPR, accessibility compliance, and sustainability before adopting anything new. Leads the team's thinking on AI in teaching and assessment. Champions assistive technology for SEND learners and advocates for the resources that reduce digital exclusion. | C1 and C2: Leader to Pioneer |

| **The Digital Lead role sits at Stage 2B to Stage 3** The Digital Lead in each curriculum area does not need to be at Stage 3, but they need to be working comfortably at Stage 2B and moving towards 3. That means they can articulate the EDS mapping for their area, they know how to run the Teams Digital Health Check, and they are a credible peer resource for colleagues still at Stage 1 or 2A. The Jisc Building Digital Capability self assessment tool is the most practical way to baseline where staff currently sit. |
| --- |

Sources: ETF Digital Teaching Professional Framework (set.et-foundation.co.uk) | DigCompEdu, European Commission (joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu/digcompedu_en) | ETF Enhance CPD Platform, free for FE staff (enhance.etfoundation.co.uk)